            "review_text": "davidtran95\nManila, Philippines87 contributions\n0\nA bowl of fish noodles soup and Pate Chaud\nJul 2014 • Family\nHu Tieu Nam Loi\nA friend reminded me of this place that I used to hang out during my youth. This Chinese noodles shop established long time ago, it must be in the 50's. All these years, I always thought the place was gone.\nThis restaurant have been passing down to several generation and continued to survive with time, with the war and with whatever came with it. The new generation is a young owner cum chef. Located at 43 Ton That Dam street, Distric 1. From the outside the place looks small, low key and does not seems interesting. You may passed by it without notice. \nThe lack of razzle dazzle is substituted with the famous \"Hu Tieu\": the Chinese white noodles soup that usually eat during breakfast, lunch and dinner. There are 2 versions : either with fish or chicken. But the stand out soup stock here is what made this noodle a place above the rest. The secret to a good bowl of noodles is a rich and flavorful soup stock. Key to a good soup stock is, chicken and pork bones that have been slowly stewed for days. The soup has to be clear, rid of impurities and not too fatty.\nAnother common practice at this place is customers liked to order the French pastry : Pate chaud to eat with the soup. They dipped it in the soup just like you do with a baguettes.\nThe set up is simple: At the entrance you will see one big pot of soup under the hot fire and another pot to boil the noodles. All other condiments: chicken, fish or beef stew, green onion, cilantro is already prepared. The service is quick. Just grab any avail metal stool, you may have to share a table, but that is part of the fun, then yelled: \" Hu tieu Ga\" (Chicken noodles) or \"Hu tieu Ca\" (Fish noodles). The bowl of steaming hot noodle will be on your face. The noodle is al dente, the shredded chicken is soft, and the soup is rich and out of this world. Order the french pastry: Pate Chaud is option. They also served Ca Phe Sua da (ice coffee) and hot tea.\n2 bowls of chicken noodles, 2 patechaud, 2 caphe sua da and hot tea for me and my bro set us back VND 236,000 or approx US 10.90. The restaurant has no ambiance and often noisy with locals but the bowl of hot noodle soup will satisfy your tummy. \nThe only thing I wish is the serving bowl should be bigger.\nRead more\nValue\nService\nFood\nWritten June 19, 2015",
